<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satake Yoshiatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Satake Shozan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satake Yoshiatsu, known by the art name Shozan, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of Kubota Domain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akita">Akita</a> from 1758 to 1785. Interested in Western learning, he invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraga-gennai">Hiraga Gennai</a> to advise on the domain's mines. Together with his retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odano-naotake">Odano Naotake</a> he developed the Akita school of painting, which combined Japanese subjects with European perspective and shading, and he wrote on painting theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/satake-yoshiatsu.jpg" alt="Satake Yoshiatsu" width="320" height="520" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satake_Yoshiatsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
